       ObjectBroker SUPPORT EXTENDED TO IBM MAINFRAMES, AS/400
 

        ...next version to offer DCE-based security, seamless
                    CORBA/COM interoperability...


Object World, SAN FRANCISCO -- Aug. 16, 1995 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation today announced the dramatic expansion of its 
ObjectBroker family with four new development and run-time 
environments -- IBM's MVS and OS/400 systems, Silicon Graphic's 
IRIX and Microsoft's Windows 95.  Digital also previewed the 
forthcoming version 2.6 of ObjectBroker, the industry's first Object 
Request Broker (ORB) to implement client/server security.  In 
addition, the company demonstrated its commitment to seamless 
CORBA/OLE interoperability and showed several third-party object-
oriented (OO) tools that ease three-tier application generation and 
integration with ObjectBroker. 

Platform Coverage Extended To IBM Mainframes 

     With today's announcement, the industry's leading family of 
CORBA-compliant object request brokers now supports 19 
hardware/operating system platforms. ObjectBroker is the first 
distributed object system to run across MVS, OS/400, OS/2 and AIX 
platforms as well as UNIX, Windows and Windows NT.  

     The release of ObjectBroker for MVS is a significant step 
forward for companies seeking to easily unlock and distribute 
mainframe data to desktop users.  Developers using ObjectBroker can 
encapsulate applications on MVS-based servers and make them 
accessible from Visual Basic and other OLE tools and applications on 
the desktop.  This virtually eliminates the need for source code 
changes to the existing mainframe applications.  Object adapters for 
CICS and IMS are planned for ObjectBroker within the next six 
months.

DCE Security Implemented

     In response to numerous customer requests, the next major 
upgrade to ObjectBroker will incorporate DCE-based security.  With 
this feature, ObjectBroker version 2.6 becomes one of the first 
secure ORB products available, employing Kerberos security, 
authentication and protection services for ObjectBroker client and 
server applications.  Digital's implementation uses the DCE standard 
Generic Security Services application programming interface 
(GSSAPI), which will permit the use of third-party Kerberos 
packages. 

     Among its new features, ObjectBroker V2.6 includes CORBA 
2.0-compliant C++ bindings and a "quick start" ease-of-use 
development facility.  The "quick start" facility processes 
interface definition language (IDL) and directly generates working 
client and server applications in a matter of minutes, significantly 
enhancing the development process.  In addition, version 2.6 
provides an implementation of the CORBA Naming Service 
specification.

     Digital also announced that the new version of ObjectBroker 
will allow remote access to unmodified CORBA object servers from 
Windows 95 and Windows NT desktops through OLE Automation, OLE 
controls and OLE custom interfaces.  Version 2.6 provides access to 
a remote CORBA object's methods as well as local desktop rendering 
of remote CORBA objects for dragging-and-dropping and linking and 
embedding in the same manner as local OLE objects.  

     "This announcement underscores Digital's commitment to 
object-based multivendor application integration based on 
standards," said Gerard van de Aast, Digital's vice president of 
Enterprise Software.  "ObjectBroker provides the best of CORBA and 
OLE integration to our customers, assuring unparalleled connections 
between the PC desktop and all of their enterprise data and 
application servers.  We're very pleased to announce that 
ObjectBroker will support the important security features required 
for distributed production application deployment."

Wells Fargo Bank Selects ObjectBroker for Enterprise Applications

     Wells Fargo Bank, based in San Francisco, selected ObjectBroker 
to implement a number of banking applications and customer-based 
services, including Internet-based consumer banking services and 
customer information systems.  

     "We selected ObjectBroker because of its extensive platform 
support and easy encapsulation capability " said Eric Castain, 
manager of Wells Direct Systems at Wells Fargo Bank.  "We've 
installed ObjectBroker on a variety of desktops and servers, and 
have been very pleased with the support from Digital and the smooth 
integration across our different platforms."

Object World Show Booth #602 Highlights Interoperability 

     Digital's booth at Object World highlights the company's 
commitment to both CORBA and the Component Object Model (COM) -- the 
object infrastructure of OLE 2.0.  In a joint demonstration with 
Hewlett-Packard and IBM, the three companies show their support for 
the CORBA V2.0 interoperability specification through an interactive 
art auction program implementing CORBA-to-CORBA interoperability.  
The display uses Digital's ObjectBroker to communicate via the CORBA 
2.0 DCE-EISOP protocol with HP's and IBM's object request brokers 
running in their booths.  The demonstration reflects Digital's 
commitment to open standards and its intent to implement CORBA 
interoperability.

     The Digital booth also features the company's COM/CORBA 
bridging technology, the basis for Digital's response to the Object 
Management Group's COM/CORBA Interworking RFP, due next week.  
Digital and Microsoft are continuing to develop the final 
specification for OLE Integration, which Digital will incorporate in 
future releases of the ObjectBroker product set.  An advance 
technology demonstration highlights OLE Integration on the UNIX 
platform.

     Also in the Digital booth are demonstrations of new 
ObjectBroker V2.6 features, including "quick start" development 
technology, IBM MVS and OS/400 support, and the enhanced OLE desktop 
connection.  A display of Internet tools working together with 
ObjectBroker provides customers and business partners with World 
Wide Web access to production systems on Digital's internal 
applications network.

Partners Demo Tools Integrated with ObjectBroker

     In the Digital booth, IDE demonstrates its popular Software 
through Pictures product linked to ObjectBroker, providing customers 
with a comprehensive solution for designing, developing and 
executing distributed object systems.  Software through Pictures 
will now automatically generate ObjectBroker IDL code from StP/OMT 
and StP/Booch models, permitting users to speed development time and 
increase the quality of CORBA-compliant distributed object systems.  
IDE also announced the porting of its newest version 2.0 of its 
entire Software through Pictures family to the Digital UNIX 
platform.  The products will be available in September.

     I-Kinetics provides reusable components for developers, using 
traditional development approaches, to access relational databases 
via the CORBA-based ObjectBroker.  I-Kinetics offers off-the-shelf 
CORBA-specific objects for accessing databases, eliminating the need 
to build them from scratch.  I-Kinetics will be one of first 
technology providers to build on and be available with ObjectBroker 
2.6.  In addition, I-Kinetics' Consulting Service Group will provide 
training and consulting services for ObjectBroker.

Pricing and Availability

     ObjectBroker prices range from approximately $5,000 for a UNIX 
development license to $150 for a PC client.  Digital now offers the 
ObjectBroker Pilot Package which provides development licenses on 
most ObjectBroker platforms, run-time licenses for pilot deployment 
on each major desktop category, complete documentation and CD-ROM 
media.  The price for the ObjectBroker Pilot Package is 
approximately $20,000. Digital also has a high volume and reseller 
purchase program including development licenses for ObjectBroker on 
MVS starting at $40,000 and run-time licenses beginning at $9,300.  
Both MVS and OS/400 versions are available today.  ObjectBroker V2.6 
will be in full customer Beta test in Fall 1995, with general product 
availability in Q1 1996.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
