                  DIGITAL SUSTAINS POSITION AMONG INDUSTRY
               PRICE/PERFORMANCE LEADERS WITH PRICE CUTS AND
                   NEW MODELS IN COMMERCIAL DESKTOP LINES
    
    
        Continues to Help Customers Transition to Pentium Technology
                    with New Levels of Price/Performance
    
    
MAYNARD, Mass. -- Aug. 23, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
moved quickly to sustain its position among PC industry leaders 
yesterday by notifying its resellers in the United States that it would
make competitive price reductions on Pentium-based models in its
commercial desktop lines. The price reductions range from 6 to 17
percent and are applicable to selected models in the company's Venturis
and Celebris GL product families. This is on top of price cuts of up
to 12 percent the company notified resellers of three weeks ago for
selected models in Digital's Venturis line. As the company continued
its support of corporate customers transitioning to Pentium technology,
Digital also announced its newest desktop PC for general business users
-- the Venturis 5133 -- and said it has begun volume shipments of its
recently introduced Venturis 5120 models. This is in addition to the 19
new Celebris GL Pentium models announced earlier this week.
  
     "This week's price moves mark the second step in a pricing action
we began a few weeks ago when we took dramatic price reductions on our
entry-level Pentium commercial desktops,' said Skip Gladfelter, vice
president of marketing, Americas, for Digital's Personal Computer
Business Unit. "Because of this aggressive price position, competition
has had farther to go recently in maintaining competitive price points. 
Today's announcements build on our widely recognized leadership in 
transitioning customers from 486 to Pentium processing."
    
Pentium Power at 486-Based Prices 

     The company reduced prices on most Pentium models in its Venturis
desktop line, designed for general business users, and on a few 16MB
models in the new Celebris GL family of high-performance desktops
announced earlier this week. Following is an overall summary of
Digital's price actions taken in the U.S. since late July:

