               DIGITAL'S TECHNOLOGY MIGRATION SERVICES
                    MAKES THE MOVE TO WINDOWS 95
                 SMOOTHER, FASTER AND LESS EXPENSIVE


MAYNARD, Mass. -- August 8, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced its Technology Migration Services for the Microsoft 
Windows 95 operating system. Companies using these new services from 
Digital will have a less expensive, less disruptive and less time-
consuming migration to the new technology than many published 
estimates suggest when provided by other service vendors. 

      With Technology Migration Services, Digital is giving its 
customers a new method of managing all technology migrations and 
upgrades. 

      Digital, also selected by Microsoft as a launch support 
partner for Windows 95, in creating its Technology Migration 
Services has customized its proven and market-tested service and 
support tools for the new operating system.  Key elements of the 
program include Assessment, Migration and Ongoing Support.  This 
effort has included investing significant incremental resources to 
train senior service representatives who already have extensive 
experience in supporting large, complex computing environments, and 
adding additional program managers.

      Digital has been a user of every beta release of Windows 95 
since October of 1994 and is migrating 15,000 people in Digital's 
own worldwide service organization to Windows 95. According to 
Cormac O'Reilly, chief information officer for Digital's Multivendor 
Customer Service Business Unit, the migration enables the Digital 
business unit to standardize on a single PC operating system while 
updating systems throughout the worldwide organization.  He also 
noted that he is "outsourcing" the work to Digital's own worldwide 
service organization and focusing his MIS people on using and 
exploiting the technology for business advantage. 

      "Many corporate Microsoft users view the move to Windows 95 as 
an ideal opportunity to upgrade and enhance their entire systems," 
said Digital vice president for Americas Multivendor Customer 
Services Janet Wallace.  "However, productivity gains and cost 
reductions associated with technology change are seldom 
instantaneous.  There is usually a transition period - or technology 
gap - when productivity slows and costs rise as people climb the 
learning curve of the new technology and phase out old systems.

      "Digital's Windows 95 support offerings create a service 
bridge across this gap. This helps customers to reduce the time 
between initial implementation and the realization of productivity 
and cost benefits while minimizing disruptions to ongoing 
operations, Wallace said."

      "Companies today are acutely aware that the price of 
introducing new technology into their business includes `hidden' 
costs that often far exceed the actual cost of the new technology 
itself," stated Jeffrey Kaplan of Dataquest.  "Digital has developed 
services that are aimed at helping customers anticipate and control 
the costs of incorporating new technologies into their day-to-day 
business operations with minimum disruption and maximum 
effectiveness."

      "Windows 95 and the Microsoft Network, for which Digital is 
the exclusive support provider worldwide, will create a tremendous 
upsurge in demand for technical support and network management 
services," Wallace noted.  "Microsoft's selection of Digital is 
strong testimony to Digital's leadership in the service and support 
of multivendor technology and mission-critical client/server 
environments."

      Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
