           DIGITAL'S WINDOWS 95 "QUICKSTART PILOT PACKAGE"
           ENABLES COMPANIES TO TEST NEW OPERATING SYSTEM


MAYNARD, Mass. -- August 8, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the "Quickstart Pilot Package" for Microsoft 
Corporation's Windows 95 operating system, a streamlined and 
inexpensive way to evaluate the new Microsoft software and Digital's 
support services before committing to a full migration. 

     The new program allows companies to migrate 100 "Windows95-ready" 
PCs to the new operating system for a fixed price of $20,000.

     "For companies that traditionally choose to wait six months or 
a year following the introduction of a new operating system before 
deciding to adopt it, Quickstart is a novel new option," said 
Digital vice president for Americas Multivendor Customer Services 
Janet Wallace. "Key users in the organization can "test drive" 
Windows 95, at minimal cost, with virtually no risk of runaway 
costs, and with comprehensive support and training."

     Under the terms of the "Quickstart" package, Digital will 
obtain the Windows 95 licenses, install the software and provide 
help desk telephone support for 90 days following installation. 
Digital also will provide training for participants in groups of 25 
and coupons for advanced technical staff training.

     To participate in the "Quickstart" program, companies must 
select users with networked systems (Novell Netware, Microsoft NT or 
Windows for Workgroups) that meet the minimum configuration 
requirements for Windows 95, including a 486DX/33Mhg PC running 
Windows 3.1 or Windows for Workgroups 3.11, 4 MB of main memory, and 
40 MB of free hard disk space.

     In conjunction with the "Quickstart" package, Digital also is 
offering optional services, including hardware/software/network 
upgrades for selected PCs that do not meet minimum configuration 
requirements.  Another option allows Digital to secure licenses for 
and install Microsoft Office 95.

     The Quickstart Pilot Package will be sold by Digital's sales 
force and through its various channel partners.

